  "articleBody": "Installing new flooring in a foreclosed property can be a great way to boost its appeal, but it's also ripe for mistakes if you're not careful. One of the biggest blunders is installing new flooring over a damaged or wet subfloor. This is a recipe for disaster; you’ll end up with buckling, warping, or even mold. Always inspect and address subfloor issues first! Another common mistake is choosing flooring materials without properly checking the property's condition or moisture levels. What works in one house might not be suitable for a foreclosed property that's had neglect or water exposure. Don't forget to budget for demolition and leveling; many folks underestimate these costs. Lastly, failing to confirm exactly what your installer will repair versus what they consider outside their scope can lead to big headaches and unexpected expenses. A good installer will provide a detailed quote that clearly outlines labor, materials, demolition, and prep work. If you're unsure about any of these steps, especially with the unpredictable nature of foreclosed homes, getting a professional like HH Remodeling LLC involved can save you from costly re-dos and ensure a solid, lasting floor.",
